**Relational schema**

```sql
CREATE TABLE customers (
  id TEXT PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT, email TEXT
);
CREATE TABLE orders (
  id TEXT PRIMARY KEY, customer_id TEXT REFERENCES customers(id),
  total_cents BIGINT, status TEXT, created_at TIMESTAMPTZ
);
CREATE TABLE order_items (
  order_id TEXT REFERENCES orders(id), sku TEXT, qty INT, price_cents BIGINT,
  PRIMARY KEY (order_id, sku)
);

-- Reconstruct the full order
SELECT o.*, json_agg(i.*) AS items, c.name
FROM orders o
JOIN order_items i ON i.order_id = o.id
JOIN customers   c ON c.id = o.customer_id
WHERE o.id = 'ord-100'
GROUP BY o.id, c.name;
```

**MongoDB document**

```javascript
// orders collection — one document carries the aggregate
{
  _id: "ord-100",
  customer: { id: "cust-42", name: "Asha Patel", email: "a@x.com" },
  items: [
    { sku: "SKU-1", qty: 2, priceCents: 1999 },
    { sku: "SKU-2", qty: 1, priceCents: 4999 }
  ],
  shippingAddress: { line1: "1 Market St", city: "SF", zip: "94105" },
  totalCents: 8997,
  status: "PLACED",
  createdAt: ISODate("2026-04-24T09:00:00Z")
}

// One round trip for the whole order
db.orders.findOne({ _id: "ord-100" });

// Enforce shape via $jsonSchema
db.runCommand({
  collMod: "orders",
  validator: {
    $jsonSchema: {
      bsonType: "object",
      required: ["_id", "customer", "items", "totalCents", "status"],
      properties: {
        totalCents: { bsonType: "long", minimum: 0 },
        status:     { enum: ["PLACED", "SHIPPED", "CANCELLED"] }
      }
    }
  },
  validationLevel: "moderate"
});
```

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    start["Model a relationship"] --> q1{"Always read together?"}
    q1 -->|"No"| ref1["Reference"]
    q1 -->|"Yes"| q2{"Bounded size? 1:1 or 1:few?"}
    q2 -->|"No — 1:many or unbounded"| q3{"Shared across many parents?"}
    q3 -->|"Yes"| ref2["Reference or Extended Reference"]
    q3 -->|"No"| ref3["Reference (bucket pattern for time-series)"]
    q2 -->|"Yes"| q4{"Child updated independently?"}
    q4 -->|"Yes"| ref4["Reference"]
    q4 -->|"No"| embed["Embed"]
```

**Embedded — order with line items**

```javascript
// orders collection (embedded items)
{
  _id: "ord-100",
  customerId: "cust-42",
  items: [
    { sku: "SKU-1", qty: 2, priceCents: 1999 },
    { sku: "SKU-2", qty: 1, priceCents: 4999 }
  ],
  totalCents: 8997,
  createdAt: ISODate("2026-04-24")
}

// Read the whole order in one round trip
db.orders.findOne({ _id: "ord-100" });
```

**Referenced — user with many comments**

```javascript
// posts collection
{ _id: "post-1", title: "Hello", authorId: "u9" }

// comments collection — potentially millions per post
{ _id: "c-1", postId: "post-1", userId: "u7", text: "Great!", t: ISODate(...) }
{ _id: "c-2", postId: "post-1", userId: "u8", text: "Same",   t: ISODate(...) }

// Paginate independently; index on { postId: 1, t: -1 }
db.comments.find({ postId: "post-1" }).sort({ t: -1 }).limit(20);
```

**Extended reference — the hybrid**

```javascript
// orders embed a denormalized customer subset, NOT the whole customer
{
  _id: "ord-100",
  customer: { id: "cust-42", name: "Asha Patel", tier: "gold" },
  items: [ ... ],
  totalCents: 8997
}

// customers collection remains the source of truth
{ _id: "cust-42", name: "Asha Patel", email: "a@x.com", tier: "gold", address: {...} }
```

**Cost of extended reference:** when the customer's name or tier changes, you either tolerate stale embedded copies or fan out an update to all orders that embed them — often via a change-stream-driven job.

**Reference pattern with a `$lookup` join**

```javascript
db.orders.aggregate([
  { $match: { _id: "ord-100" } },
  { $lookup: {
      from:         "customers",
      localField:   "customerId",
      foreignField: "_id",
      as:           "customer"
  }},
  { $unwind: "$customer" }
]);
```

**Performance notes**
- `$lookup` is slower than an embedded read — it is a join, and MongoDB isn't optimized for join-heavy workloads
- Avoid deeply nested `$lookup` in hot aggregation paths
- On sharded collections, only the `from` side can be sharded (in modern versions); check your server version's constraints
- Indexed `foreignField` is essential
